From 534c56b8b131c8b1070f573674f5347a4d05c8f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex Executor <codex-executor@local>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 13:12:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/22] carrier: cube-symmetric (Union Jack) port triangulation

The rotation check showed a 3 % low-frequency residual between a cell and
its cube-symmetry image, unchanged by mesh refinement.  A same-mesh
experiment (the original Tet mesh rotated rigidly and labelled against the
image cell's carrier, so the fine discretisation is identical on both
sides) put the residual at 3.5 %: it is the carrier, not the mesh.

Cause: the frozen carrier splits every square by fanning from the corner
with the smallest node id, and that id is a hash of the exact coordinates,
so each square's diagonal is drawn pseudo-randomly.  47 of the 48 cube
symmetries map the triangulation onto a different one, and the Schur
complement is expressed in that P1 space.

carrier_triangulation.unionjack_layout rebuilds the triangulation of the
complete lattice squares with a rule that is exactly invariant for even
carrier resolution: join the two corners whose lattice coordinate sum is
even (each square has exactly two, diagonally opposite; a cube symmetry
maps a coordinate c to c or n - c, preserving parity).  Node identity,
node count and the local-to-global maps are untouched, and the patches
clipped by the cut plane keep the frozen fan.  The vendored generator is a
frozen SHA-256 bound snapshot, so this is a post-processing step in this
route's package rather than an edit of the vendor.

With the symmetric carrier the same-mesh residual is 7.5e-15: the rotation
action on the label is exact at machine precision.  Unit test covers the
invariance under all 48 symmetries; sheet_rotation_check gains
--carrier-triangulation.
